les. Release timing coordination (default-on ships with GA) is a release-composition matter, noted as an AC/coordination note on E004, not a DAG dependency. |\n| E003 ∥ E004 (non-edge) | Rule 14 | Different components, teams, and repos; fully independent. |\n\n**Rule 19 considered and not applied:** the strategy is a GA graduation\nprogram, not a safety-critical implementation — the guardrails work (HLR-4)\nqualifies an existing TrustyAI integration (TrustyAI itself is Stable/GA per\nits architecture-context file) rather than building new guardrails, sandboxing,\nor RBAC. No separate fail-mode/security Investigations are generated.\n\n**Rule 23 (rollback) applied as ACs:** E004 replaces the existing TP opt-in\ngate behavior and E003 replaces the experimental-flag behavior; both carry a\n\"revertible via supported configuration, no code changes\" acceptance criterion.\n\n**Component mapping notes:** E003's work spans TrustyAI and the Llama Stack\ndistribution configuration surface; it is assigned the canonical component\n`TrustyAI` (the guardrails capability and its qualification are owned by the\nTrustyAI team, and TrustyAI is the distinct affected component/team in the\nstrategy). E005 is assigned `Llama Stack` — the documentation and support\nposture are for the Llama Stack GA. All component values are verbatim from\n`.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n\n**Team attribution note:** the strategy has no Impacted Teams table (see\nHealth Warnings); teams are taken from the architecture-context owning-team\nfields — RHOAI GenAI (Llama Stack), TrustyAI (TrustyAI), RHOAI Dashboard\n(Dashboard).\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | The GA-readiness assessment itself: inventory of gaps (API stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, error handling) producing the definitive GA scope. The remediation of the GA-blocking findings it identifies is executed in RHAISTRAT-9014-E002. |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Versioned APIs, deprecation policy, and tested upgrade path from the last two TP releases are one stability-guarantee deliverable on the published API surface; the deprecation policy is authored as part of the versioning work (same surface, same logical change — splitting it into the docs epic would sever the policy from the versioning decisions it expresses). |\n| HLR-3 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Support posture documentation (SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix) is substantive content authoring — a separate docs-authoring epic per the work-product boundary rule, not bundled into the hardening epic. |\n| HLR-4 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Distinct component (TrustyAI) and team; independent integration surface, deferrable relative to the P0 stability work — separate epic per the epic boundary and priority-split rules. |\n| HLR-5 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | Distinct component (Dashboard) and team; independent user-facing change — separate epic. |\n| HLR-6 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 (bundled) | Bundled with HLR-3 in the GA documentation epic: both are Llama Stack documentation authoring for the same doc surface, same team, same work-product type (docs-authoring). HLR-6 is not independently deferrable here — splitting it out would create two docs-authoring epics over the same documentation surface with an artificial serialization edge between them, and under the docs-authoring priority exception both would derive P0 from the strategy's Critical priority anyway, so a priority split buys nothing. Migration notes and support posture publish together as one GA documentation set. |\n\nAll six HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy provides no\n  Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance\n  criteria, no Non-Functional Requirements, and no Staff Engineer Input; the\n  Affected Components section is a bare list without change descriptions or\n  owner teams. Teams were inferred from architecture-context owning-team\n  fields, and epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs. Human\n  verification of team attribution recommended.\n- **Sizing estimate is unstable until the assessment lands**: the strategy's\n  open question states the stability/hardening size is an estimate until the\n  GA-readiness assessment produces the definitive scope. This is managed, not\n  just flagged: E002 carries `gated_by=E001` with\n  `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` and a fallback approach that re-derives\n  its scope from the assessment report. A concrete re-plan trigger: if the\n  assessment's validated gap inventory materially exceeds the strategy's\n  implied scope (e.g., GA-blocking findings requiring changes beyond the\n  published API surface and error handling), re-split E002 along the\n  assessment's dimension boundaries.\n- **Priority note on the docs epic**: E005 is P0 via the docs-authoring\n  priority exception (strategy priority Critical → P0) even though it carries\n  the P2 HLR-6 alongside the P0 HLR-3; it does not push priority upstream —\n  E003 and E004 remain P1 per their HLRs.\n- **Scope trap watched**: HLR-3's \"supported configuration matrix\" implies\n  validated configurations that only exist after the hardening work; this is\n  why the docs epic is blocked by all Implementation epics (Rule 11) rather\n  than authored ahead of them.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable: no external dependency with uncertain timing. The single\nunknown (GA scope) is internal to the strategy and handled by the E001 → E002\ngate.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":36416,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9bfc3c559ea49f44","agent_id":"ab5c1054f47f28962","session_id":"30fb2c83-c81f-4c8e-a80f-08c43025fc68","uuid":"3fb09319-93ca-4216-adfa-5ad