{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c9b81ced060c9dc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: Completeness\n9\t  description: DAG justification claims E004 carries a release-timing coordination\n10\t    AC/note for GA graduation, but E004 has no such note in its scope or acceptance\n11\t    criteria — add the coordination note to E004 (or drop the claim from the summary)\n12\terror: null\n13\t---\n14\t## Review Summary\n15\t\n16\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n17\tRecommendation: accept\n18\t\n19\tContract validator: OK (all mechanical checks passed — body sections, canonical\n20\tcomponent names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance,\n21\timplementation-type parallelism). Triage consistency: `triage` is null (full\n22\tdecomposition); 5 epics is the expected shape. Graded below is only what the\n23\tvalidator cannot check.\n24\t\n25\t## Criterion Details\n26\t\n27\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n28\tAll six HLRs map to epics: HLR-1 → E001 (assessment) with the remediation half\n29\texplicitly executed in E002; HLR-2 → E002; HLR-3 → E005; HLR-4 → E003;\n30\tHLR-5 → E004; HLR-6 → E005 (bundled). The HLR-6 bundling is compliant: it\n31\tshares the documentation surface, team, and work-product type with HLR-3 (not a\n32\tdistinct deferrable feature), and the matrix row carries the `(bundled)` marker\n33\twith inline rationale. The HLR-2 deprecation-policy placement in E002 (rather\n34\tthan the docs epic) is recorded with rationale in the matrix row — same\n35\tsurface, same logical change. Priority inheritance is clean: E001 (P0) blocks\n36\tonly P0 work; E003/E004 (P1) block only E005, which is a docs-authoring epic\n37\texempt from priority inheritance (its P0 derives from the strategy's Critical\n38\tpriority, correctly explained in Health Warnings).\n39\t\n40\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n41\tNo circular dependencies; all edges valid. Cross-check of diagram vs\n42\tfrontmatter: E002 depends on [E001], E005 on [E002, E003, E004] — exactly the\n43\tfour diagram edges, arrow direction dependency → dependent as convention\n44\trequires. E001 → E002 is a justified investigation gate (the strategy's only\n45\topen question states the GA scope depends on the assessment), with `gated_by`\n46\tpresent in E002's direct dependencies. E002/E003/E004 → E005 are Rule 11\n47\tdocs-last edges. Non-edges are correctly justified: the assessment's enumerated\n48\tdimensions (API stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, error handling) do not\n49\tcover guardrails or Dashboard enablement, so E001 ∥ E003/E004 is right; E003\n50\tand E004 are different components, teams, and repos. No konflux-onboarding,\n51\tlicense-validation, or repo-onboarding epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism\n52\tis not in play. Critical path length 3 (E001 → E002 → E005) fits the M-standard\n53\theuristic (3-4). No data/artifact dependency hidden in epic content is missing\n54\tfrom the DAG: E005's configuration-matrix consistency is served by the\n55\tE002 → E005 edge, and E003's integration documentation is internal to E003's\n56\town deliverable.\n57\t\n58\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n59\tEach epic is a single component/team tuple with a canonical component name\n60\tverbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt` (Llama Stack, TrustyAI, Dashboard).\n61\tE003 spans the TrustyAI integration and the Llama Stack distribution\n62\tconfiguration surface, but this is one logical change (promoting the\n63\tintegration to supported) owned by one team — the summary records the\n64\tcomponent-mapping rationale, and TrustyAI is the correct canonical assignment.\n65\tE002 bundles the stability-guarantee deliverables of HLR-2 plus the\n66\tassessment-driven remediation; this is the strategy's own framing of one work\n67\titem, and the Health Warning carries a concrete conditional re-split trigger\n68\t(re-split along assessment dimension boundaries if the validated inventory\n69\texceeds the estimate) — the compliant shape, watch note only, no split demanded.\n70\tNo scope duplication between E003's integration documentation and E005's GA\n71\tdocumentation set — E003 explicitly scopes its docs to the integration\n72\tdeliverable and distinguishes them from the broader uplift. No work-product\n73\ttype mixing: E005 is pure docs-authoring; the deprecation policy in E002 is\n74\tpart of the HLR-2 stability-guarantee deliverable (the policy expresses the\n75\tversioning decisions), not curated content authoring.\n76\t\n77\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n78\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (the definitive GA scope) changes\n79\twhat E002 does, and E002 carries `gated_by=E001` with both `action` and\n80\t`fallback_approach` populated. Every `gated_by` target appears in the epic's\n81\tdirect `dependencies` (E002's only dependency is E001). No other epic lists an\n82\tInvestigation in `dependencies`, so no missing `gated_by`. All Implementation\n83\tepics produce concrete artifacts (versioned API surface + remediations,\n84\tqualified integration, default-on Dashboard behavior, published docs). Type\n85\tassignment of E005 as Implementation with `implementation_type:\n86\tdocs-authoring` is correct. No misclassifications.\n87\t\n88\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n89\tEach epic is scored with the rubric matching its type: E001 carries\n90\t`investigation_signals` (5-signal), E002–E005 carry `ai_signals` (9-signal) —\n91\tno cross-type contamination. Signal values are consistent with the rationales\n92\tand the strategy content: E002's `open_questions: -1` matches its direct gating\n93\tby the assessment; E003/E004's `open_questions: 1` match the justified\n94\tnon-edges from E001; E001's `human_judgment_required: -1` and\n95\t`cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` correctly mark peripheral (not gating)\n96\tneeds. Calibration check on `architecture_clai