{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-818499626eb26100","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9014: Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability\n10\t\n11\tStrategy status: Refinement Done. Strategy priority: Critical (frontmatter).\n12\tAll three affected components (Llama Stack, TrustyAI, Dashboard) have\n13\tarchitecture-context files and are in the platform; no onboarding chains are\n14\tneeded (Llama Stack is already Konflux-onboarded per its context file).\n15\t\n16\t## Epic List\n17\t\n18\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n19\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | GA-readiness assessment for Llama Stack | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Llama Stack API stability and GA hardening | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Supported TrustyAI guardrails integration for Llama Stack distributions | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | Dashboard Llama Stack management enabled by default for entitled clusters | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Llama Stack GA documentation and production support posture | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n25\t\n26\t## Dependency DAG\n27\t\n28\t```mermaid\n29\tgraph TD\n30\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 GA-readiness assessment] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 API stability and hardening]\n31\t    E002 --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 GA docs and support posture]\n32\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 TrustyAI guardrails integration] --> E005\n33\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 Dashboard default enablement] --> E005\n34\t```\n35\t\n36\t## DAG Justification\n37\t\n38\t| Edge / non-edge | Rule | Rationale |\n39\t|-----------------|------|-----------|\n40\t| E001 → E002 | Investigation edge (Rule 3) | The strategy's only open question states the GA scope depends on the assessment — until it lands, the size of the stability and hardening work is an estimate. The assessment's answer changes what E002 does, so E002 is a true gate (`gated_by=E001`, `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`). Outcomes are an unbounded gap inventory (not ≤3 bounded possibilities), so no conditional branch epics; the gate is expressed via `gate_failure_impact` on E002. |\n41\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | GA documentation and the support posture (SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix, migration notes) describe what was built; blocked by every Implementation epic. |\n42\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same — the GA documentation set must reflect the supported guardrails integration. |\n43\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same — the GA documentation set must reflect the default-on Dashboard management experience. |\n44\t| E001 ∥ E003 (non-edge) | Rule 14 / Step 3 decision rule | The assessment's scope is enumerated as API stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, and error handling — it does not cover the TrustyAI guardrails integration. Whether guardrails are promoted to a supported integration does not depend on the assessment outcome, so no gate. |\n45\t| E001 ∥ E004 (non-edge) | Rule 14 / Step 3 decision rule | Dashboard default enablement is not part of the assessment's enumerated dimensions; the answer does not change what the Dashboard epic does. |\n46\t| E002 ∥ E003 (non-edge) | Rule 14 | Different components, different teams, different repos (llama-stack midstream vs. trustyai-service-operator); no shared build artifacts. The guardrails integration qualifies against the current distribution and does not build against the API-stability work. |\n47\t| E002 ∥ E004 (non-edge) | Rules 14/15 | Different components and repos; the Dashboard change is a feature-flag/default flip on an existing management surface, not work that builds against the API stability deliverables. Release timing coordination (default-on ships with GA) is a release-composition matter, noted as an AC/coordination note on E004, not a DAG dependency. |\n48\t| E003 ∥ E004 (non-edge) | Rule 14 | Different components, teams, and repos; fully independent. |\n49\t\n50\t**Rule 19 considered and not applied:** the strategy is a GA graduation\n51\tprogram, not a safety-critical implementation — the guardrails work (HLR-4)\n52\tqualifies an existing TrustyAI integration (TrustyAI itself is Stable/GA per\n53\tits architecture-context file) rather than building new guardrails, sandboxing,\n54\tor RBAC. No separate fail-mode/security Investigations are generated.\n55\t\n56\t**Rule 23 (rollback) applied as ACs:** E004 replaces the existing TP opt-in\n57\tgate behavior and E003 replaces the experimental-flag behavior; both carry a\n58\t\"revertible via supported configuration, no code changes\" acceptance criterion.\n59\t\n60\t**Component mapping notes:** E003's work spans TrustyAI and the Llama Stack\n61\tdistribution configuration surface; it is assigned the canonical component\n62\t`TrustyAI` (the guardrails capability and its qualification are owned by the\n63\tTrustyAI team, and TrustyAI is the distinct affected component/team in the\n64\tstrategy). E005 is assigned `Llama Stack` — the documentation and support\n65\tposture are for the Llama Stack GA. All component values are verbatim from\n66\t`.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n67\t\n68\t**Team attribution note:** the strategy has no Impacted Teams table (see\n69\tHealth Warnings); teams are taken from the architecture-context owning-team\n70\tfields — RHOAI GenAI (Llama Stack), TrustyAI (TrustyAI), RHOAI Dashboard\n71\t(Dashboard).\n72\t\n73\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n74\t\n75\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n76\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n77\t| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | The GA-readiness assessment itself: invent