{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a5192f9e24f05a1c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9014: Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability\n11\t\n12\tStrategy status: Refinement Done. Strategy priority: Critical (frontmatter).\n13\tAll three affected components (Llama Stack, TrustyAI, Dashboard) have\n14\tarchitecture-context files and are in the platform; no onboarding chains are\n15\tneeded (Llama Stack is already Konflux-onboarded per its context file).\n16\t\n17\t## Epic List\n18\t\n19\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n20\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | GA-readiness assessment for Llama Stack | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Llama Stack API stability and GA hardening | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Supported TrustyAI guardrails integration for Llama Stack distributions | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | Dashboard Llama Stack management enabled by default for entitled clusters | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Llama Stack GA documentation and production support posture | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n26\t\n27\t## Dependency DAG\n28\t\n29\t```mermaid\n30\tgraph TD\n31\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 GA-readiness assessment] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 API stability and hardening]\n32\t    E002 --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 GA docs and support posture]\n33\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 TrustyAI guardrails integration] --> E005\n34\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 Dashboard default enablement] --> E005\n35\t```\n36\t\n37\t## DAG Justification\n38\t\n39\t| Edge / non-edge | Rule | Rationale |\n40\t|-----------------|------|-----------|\n41\t| E001 → E002 | Investigation edge (Rule 3) | The strategy's only open question states the GA scope depends on the assessment — until it lands, the size of the stability and hardening work is an estimate. The assessment's answer changes what E002 does, so E002 is a true gate (`gated_by=E001`, `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`). Outcomes are an unbounded gap inventory (not ≤3 bounded possibilities), so no conditional branch epics; the gate is expressed via `gate_failure_impact` on E002. |\n42\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | GA documentation and the support posture (SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix, migration notes) describe what was built; blocked by every Implementation epic. |\n43\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same — the GA documentation set must reflect the supported guardrails integration. |\n44\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same — the GA documentation set must reflect the default-on Dashboard management experience. |\n45\t| E001 ∥ E003 (non-edge) | Rule 14 / Step 3 decision rule | The assessment's scope is enumerated as API stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, and error handling — it does not cover the TrustyAI guardrails integration. Whether guardrails are promoted to a supported integration does not depend on the assessment outcome, so no gate. |\n46\t| E001 ∥ E004 (non-edge) | Rule 14 / Step 3 decision rule | Dashboard default enablement is not part of the assessment's enumerated dimensions; the answer does not change what the Dashboard epic does. |\n47\t| E002 ∥ E003 (non-edge) | Rule 14 | Different components, different teams, different repos (llama-stack midstream vs. trustyai-service-operator); no shared build artifacts. The guardrails integration qualifies against the current distribution and does not build against the API-stability work. |\n48\t| E002 ∥ E004 (non-edge) | Rules 14/15 | Different components and repos; the Dashboard change is a feature-flag/default flip on an existing management surface, not work that builds against the API stability deliverables. Release timing coordination (default-on ships with GA) is a release-composition matter, noted as a coordination note in E004's Notes section, not a DAG dependency. |\n49\t| E003 ∥ E004 (non-edge) | Rule 14 | Different components, teams, and repos; fully independent. |\n50\t\n51\t**Rule 19 considered and not applied:** the strategy is a GA graduation\n52\tprogram, not a safety-critical implementation — the guardrails work (HLR-4)\n53\tqualifies an existing TrustyAI integration (TrustyAI itself is Stable/GA per\n54\tits architecture-context file) rather than building new guardrails, sandboxing,\n55\tor RBAC. No separate fail-mode/security Investigations are generated.\n56\t\n57\t**Rule 23 (rollback) applied as ACs:** E004 replaces the existing TP opt-in\n58\tgate behavior and E003 replaces the experimental-flag behavior; both carry a\n59\t\"revertible via supported configuration, no code changes\" acceptance criterion.\n60\t\n61\t**Component mapping notes:** E003's work spans TrustyAI and the Llama Stack\n62\tdistribution configuration surface; it is assigned the canonical component\n63\t`TrustyAI` (the guardrails capability and its qualification are owned by the\n64\tTrustyAI team, and TrustyAI is the distinct affected component/team in the\n65\tstrategy). E005 is assigned `Llama Stack` — the documentation and support\n66\tposture are for the Llama Stack GA. All component values are verbatim from\n67\t`.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n68\t\n69\t**Team attribution note:** the strategy has no Impacted Teams table (see\n70\tHealth Warnings); teams are taken from the architecture-context owning-team\n71\tfields — RHOAI GenAI (Llama Stack), TrustyAI (TrustyAI), RHOAI Dashboard\n72\t(Dashboard).\n73\t\n74\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n75\t\n76\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n77\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n78\t| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | The GA-readiness assessment itself: inventory of gaps (API stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, error handling) producing the definitive GA scope. The remediation of the GA-blocking findings it identifies is executed in RHAISTRAT-9014-E002. |\n79\t| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Versioned APIs, deprecation policy, and tested upgrade path from the last two TP releases are one stability-guarantee deliverable on the published API surface; the deprecation policy is authored as part of the versioning work (same surface, same logical change — splitting it into the docs epic would sever the policy from the versioning decisions it expresses). |\n80\t| HLR-3 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Support posture documentation (SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix) is substantive content authoring — a separate docs-authoring epic per the work-product boundary rule, not bundled into the hardening epic. |\n81\t| HLR-4 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Distinct component (TrustyAI) and team; independent integration surface, deferrable relative to the P0 stability work — separate epic per the epic boundary and priority-split rules. |\n82\t| HLR-5 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | Distinct component (Dashboard) and team; independent user-facing change — separate epic. |\n83\t| HLR-6 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 (bundled) | Bundled with HLR-3 in the GA documentation epic: both are Llama Stack documentation authoring for the same doc surface, same team, same work-product type (docs-authoring). HLR-6 is not independently deferrable here — splitting it out would create two docs-authoring epics over the same documentation surface with an artificial serialization edge between them, and under the docs-authoring priority exception both would derive P0 from the strategy's Critical priority anyway, so a priority split buys nothing. Migration notes and support posture publish together as one GA documentation set. |\n84\t\n85\tAll six HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n86\t\n87\t## Health Warnings\n88\t\n89\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy provides no\n90\t  Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance\n91\t  criteria, no Non-Functional Requirements, and no Staff Engineer Input; the\n92\t  Affected Components section is a bare list without change descriptions or\n93\t  owner teams. Teams were inferred from architecture-context owning-team\n94\t  fields, and epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs. Human\n95\t  verification of team attribution recommended.\n96\t- **Sizing estimate is unstable until the assessment lands**: the strategy's\n97\t  open question states the stability/hardening size is an estimate until the\n98\t  GA-readiness assessment produces the definitive scope. This is managed, not\n99\t  just flagged: E002 carries `gated_by=E001` with\n100\t  `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` and a fallback approach that re-derives\n101\t  its scope from the assessment report. A concrete re-plan trigger: if the\n102\t  assessment's validated gap inventory materially exceeds the strategy's\n103\t  implied scope (e.g., GA-blocking findings requiring changes beyond the\n104\t  published API surface and error handling), re-split E002 along the\n105\t  assessment's dimension boundaries.\n106\t- **Priority note on the docs epic**: E005 is P0 via the docs-authoring\n107\t  priority exception (strategy priority Critical → P0) even though it carries\n108\t  the P2 HLR-6 alongside the P0 HLR-3; it does not push priority upstream —\n109\t  E003 and E004 remain P1 per their HLRs.\n110\t- **Scope trap watched**: HLR-3's \"supported configuration matrix\" implies\n111\t  validated configurations that only exist after the hardening work; this is\n112\t  why the docs epic is blocked by all Implementation epics (Rule 11) rather\n113\t  than authored ahead of them.\n114\t\n115\t## Tiered Delivery\n116\t\n117\tNot applicable: no external dependency with uncertain timing. The single\n118\tunknown (GA scope) is internal to the strategy and handled by the E001 → E002\n119\tgate.\n120\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e07987742a7e0e3","agent_id":"a782a49403a33104d","session_id":"30fb2c83-c81f-4c8e-a80f-08c43025fc68","uuid":"9d840f5c-755a-4c84-9a99-bd7b729cc11d","timestamp":"2026-10-26T12:15:41.680Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a5192f9e24f05a1c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9014"}
